## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of multi-tenant scheduling, latency guarantees, and dynamic resource control under pressure.

## Key Concepts
- Latency-aware scheduling across containers
- Per-container quotas and dynamic weights
- Runnable vs blocked time accounting
- Preemption policy and fairness under contention

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct {
  int id;
  uint64_t runnable_ns;
  uint64_t blocked_ns;
  uint64_t cpu_quota_ns;
  uint64_t base_weight;
  // ... other fields
} container_t;

static inline uint64_t total_time(container_t *c){
  return c->runnable_ns + c->blocked_ns;
}

void update_weight(container_t *c){
  uint64_t t = total_time(c);
  if (t == 0) t = 1;
  c->weight = (uint64_t)(c->base_weight * c->runnable_ns / t);
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate latency under mixed UI and batch workloads? 
- How would you adapt the policy for sudden memory pressure spikes or NUMA effects?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests concrete understanding of how 2-level paging translates addresses and handles missing pages, a core OS concept.

## Key Concepts
- 2-level paging and index computation
- Present bit and frame number extraction
- On-demand page allocation via a create flag

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal walk in C-like pseudocode
uint32_t* walk(uint32_t* root, uint32_t va, int create){
  uint32_t l1 = (va >> 22) & 0x3FF;
  uint32_t l2 = (va >> 12) & 0x3FF;
  uint32_t offset = va & 0xFFF;
  uint32_t* pte1 = root + l1;
  if (!(*pte1 & 0x1)) { // not present
    if (!create) return NULL;
    uint32_t* newl2 = alloc_page();
    *pte1 = ((uint32_t)newl2) | 0x1; // mark present
  }
  uint32_t* l2tbl = (uint32_t*)(*pte1 & ~0xFFF);
  uint32_t* pte2 = l2tbl + l2;
  return pte2;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would TLB misses interact with this walk and how would you invalidate entries after a page remap?
- How would you extend to support lazy allocation of both levels on demand and handle concurrent faults safely?

